<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vijnaneshvara</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">11th–12th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vijnaneshvara was an Indian jurist of the late 11th and early 12th centuries who lived at the court of the Western Chalukya king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramaditya-vi">Vikramaditya VI</a> at Kalyani. His <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitakshara">Mitakshara</a>, a commentary on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yajnavalkya">Yajnavalkya</a> Smriti, became the leading authority on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> law across most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, especially on inheritance and family property, and was later applied by British courts.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitakshara">Mitakshara</a>, Vijnaneshvara lived during the reign of the Chalukya king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramaditya-vi">Vikramaditya VI</a> (r. 1076–1126), who ruled from Kalyana. He belonged to the Bharadvaja gotra, his father was named Padmanabha-bhatta, and he was the pupil of Uttama and a student of the Purva-mimamsa school. The scholar J. Duncan M. Derrett speculates that he was a judge, but P. V. Kane says there is no evidence for this. In a verse the author calls himself a pandita, while the colophons of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a> describe him as an ascetic and renunciant. Kane suggests that he was not an ascetic when he began the work but became one shortly before or after finishing it.</p><p>An inscription of 1123–1124 at the Sri Kalingeshwara Temple in Martur, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalaburagi">Kalaburagi</a> district, names a Vijnanesvara-Bhattaraka, who appears to have been an adviser and guru of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramaditya-vi">Vikramaditya VI</a>. It says he was born at Maseyamadu, that his original name was Kancha, and that the king held him in high regard and touched his feet in reverence.</p><p>Historians including P. B. Desai, V. S. Kulkarni and J. M. Nagaiah identify him with the author of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitakshara">Mitakshara</a>. Sitaram Jagirdar and M. M. Kalburgi dispute this, since the inscription gives his father as Soma-raja and his gotra as Kaushika. Kane thinks Soma-raja could be another name of Padmanabha, but finds no concrete evidence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Vijnaneshvara wrote the Ṛjumitākṣarā, better known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitakshara">Mitakshara</a>, a detailed commentary on the Yājñavalkya Smṛti that became an authoritative text on law in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. Kane places its composition between 1100 and 1120, reasoning that so large a work took several years, while Derrett assigns it to 1121–1125, a dating Kane considers wrong.</p><p>He also appears to have written the Ashaucha-dashaka, or Dasha-shloki, a treatise on ritual impurity. Hari-hara, probably his pupil, wrote a commentary on it, though some sources hold that Hari-hara wrote the work and Vijnaneshvara the commentary. The Trimshat Shloki is also attributed to him, but this is highly doubtful. His student Narayana wrote the Vyavahara-shiromani, a digest on judicial procedure and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>The Vijñāneśvara Pratisthana Trust, founded by the politician and judge Rama Jois, established a research and study centre, Vijñāneśvara Bhavan, at Martur in 2004. The Karnataka State Government gave ₹ 10 million to develop the six-acre Vijnaneshwara Park beside it and set up the Vijñāneśvara Government Law College at Martur, later moved to the Gulbarga University campus.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vijñāneśvara” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Someshvara II, introduced the Chalukya-Vikrama era to date records from his accession and presided over a court that included the Kashmiri poet Bilhana, author of the Vikramankadevacharita, and the jurist <b>Vijnaneshvara</b>, whose Mitakshara commentary on the law code of Yajnavalkya became a leading authority on Hindu inheritance law.</blockquote><a href="/empire/western-chalukya-empire">Western Chalukya Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
